      "math_academy_id": "q-75187",
      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=(x-4)({x}^{2}+3)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)",
      "worked_solution": "The equation is already fully factored. Note that the discriminant of ${x}^{2}+3=0$ is \n\n$$\nD=(0)^{2}-4(1)(3)=-12<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the function $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=4.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0-4)({0}^{2}+3) \\\\  & =(-4)(3) \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at the point $(0,-12),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=4,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14240558/assets/310ba3049a0a91f23259a67eb3c0606749f6a83a75151d6b0d41dfc4e98706d1.png)",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
      "result": "Correct",
